./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/TASK-260728-3a2dnr_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-e2199c.log:3292:scope:In scope: pinned libssh2 and crypto backend; extension-safe static or source build; session handshake; raw host-key access before authentication; approved public-key auth; direct-tcpip; bidirectional exec and exec/stdin upload; client-initiated rekey trigger; nonblocking EAGAIN integration; bounded buffers; deterministic cancellation and timeouts; keepalive; cheaply-available metrics; privacy-safe error mapping; allocator hooks where supported; macOS harness registration; macOS provider configuration build. Out of scope: iOS simulator and iPhone provider configurations (iOS deferred); consumer-driven receive-window credit semantics, RFC channel-open reason taxonomy, exact exec-exit metadata, and deep rekey/keepalive observability (all owned by the M3 task); NIOSSH fork work; masking an unsupported viability gate without a recorded decision; blocking calls on provider executors; SFTP; password prompts; ProxyJump; agent forwarding; production lane scheduling; candidate types outside the adapter.
./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/TASK-260728-3a2dnr_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-e2199c.log:3293:ac:1. The adapter builds from pinned source with the approved crypto backend for the macOS harness and the macOS provider configuration and exposes no libssh2 type across the shared boundary. 2. Socket readiness and LIBSSH2_ERROR_EAGAIN are integrated without busy spinning or unbounded buffering, with deterministic cancellation and timeout behavior. 3. Raw host-key evidence before authentication, public-key auth, direct-tcpip, bidirectional exec, exec/stdin upload, EOF and half-close, keepalive, available metrics, and privacy-safe errors conform or are marked red with reproducible evidence. 4. Client-initiated rekey by byte and time threshold is proven through supported APIs of the accepted fork; the four M3-deferred semantics are emitted as explicit not-reported or unsupported states and are traceable to TASK-260728-3cveay. 5. Repeated connect and cancel smoke runs return sessions, channels, sockets, tasks, and custom allocations to baseline.
./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/TASK-260728-3a2dnr_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-e2199c.log:3295:id:TASK-260728-3cveay
./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/TASK-260728-3a2dnr_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-e2199c.log:3300:scope:In scope: consumer-driven receive-window credit with an immutable per-channel cap that is not auto-adjusted before consumer delivery; RFC 4254 channel-open rejection reason taxonomy preserved end to end; exact exec-exit metadata distinguishing status 0 from absent metadata and exposing exit-signal coreDumped; deep rekey and keepalive observability including server-initiated KEX lifecycle/generation and reply-correlated keepalive RTT, timeout, and miss metrics; the fork or upstream change needed on the selected engine plus its maintenance and rebase owner; conformance tests and harness evidence. Out of scope: re-opening the M0 engine selection, NIOSSH fork work while libssh2 remains primary, lane scheduling policy, and hardcoding numeric window or rekey values outside the accepted memory ledger.
./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/TASK-260728-3a2dnr_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--codex-_RUN-260728-e2199c.log:3301:ac:1. Each of the four deferred semantics is implemented or explicitly recorded red with pinned-source evidence; no semantic is silently dropped. 2. Receive-window credit is consumer-driven with an immutable cap proven by a test that stalls the consumer and observes no credit return. 3. Channel-open rejection reasons, exec exit status/notReported/coreDumped, and server-rekey generation plus keepalive RTT/timeout/miss are asserted against a real SSH host, not mocks. 4. Every added buffer, window, and metric fits the accepted TASK-260715-1pn983 memory ledger and the task reports measured footprint deltas. 5. Any engine fork records exact pinned revision, patch scope, security-monitoring, and rebase owner.
